hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in the beginning  of metaphysics book 1 Aristotle writes  one of his most famous statements that  is all human beings by nature desire to  know or knowledge and there's a lot of  different ways in which we can talk  about knowing and knowledge a lot of  different Arts and Sciences  there's also experience but one of them  is particularly important for Aristotle  he is a philosopher and he's also going  to tell us about the origin of  philosophy which according to him lies  in trying to develop trying to attain  knowledge motivated by something in  particular an experience and here's  where we get another one of his  extremely famous lines he says  philosophy begins in wandering or in  wonder or aw you could translate this in  a number of different ways how Maude's  Ain the verb is the Greek term that's  being used there and it means another  way of translating it to Marvel so we do  desire knowledge and some things within  the  world draw us in further now Aristotle  thinks that this this working of human  beings because of their very nature and  their capabilities doesn't lead us to  philosophy right at the very beginning  so he wouldn't say that you know early  human beings are all philosophers or  children by their very you know origin  are automatically philosophers or  anything of that sort because for him  philosophy is a bit more advanced you  could say of an inquiry he does tell us  early on about the development of the  arts and the sciences the techn I and  the epistle  in Greek and he tells us that first we  human beings after we get beyond the  realm of mere memory and experience and  we actually start to figure out how  things work we start to develop bodies  of knowledge or disciplines we do so  motivated first by practical concerns so  we develop those as he says that are  useful for life for our living for  preserving life for living better some  of these deal with things that he calls  necessities time an kaya the things that  we are bound to deal with so you know if  we are say herders we start to figure  out something about how to take herd  animals and get them safely from point A  to point B with losing as few of them as  possible or we start to figure out what  to do with their hides or their wool and  we develop these techniques that  eventually we can have some actual  knowledge about and teach to other  people  some of them deal with what we can  translate as pastimes or he also says at  another point with pleasure so quite a  few of the arts and sciences that we  develop after we get past the realm of  mere necessity have to do with making  our life more enjoyable  so think about Agra  culture for example it's really useful  to know how irrigation works and about  you know weeding and maneuvering and all  these other things that we can you know  talk about as part of the body of  knowledge of Agriculture and then we  could start to breed things to make them  more tasty melons you know were popular  in the ancient world cucumbers were  another thing as well that he could  could have referred to because they were  bred very early but he's not  particularly interested in that neither  are we  well we're really interested in is other  kinds of studies studies that are not  being carried out for their practical  effects although they could have some  practical utility but are being carried  out by a pure desire for knowledge  knowledge for the sake of knowing  theoretical as he's going to call it  knowledge as opposed to practical  leisure is what makes this possible so  he talks for example about the Egyptians  and developing the rudiments of  mathematics because they had a class of  the priests who were actually leisured  and able to start studying this for its  own sake not just for the sake of  dividing up land equally but to think  about numbers and so we we then have  studies that we carry out for the sake  of knowing we start doing them because  we value knowledge for its own sake not  just because it'll help us plow the  fields better or create more grain or  have you know straighter lines or  anything like that but because we want  to know now here's where we get into the  the famous line about philosophy having  its origin in wonder or in amazement or  marveling he tells us that through  wonder dia told thum thum insane people  begin to any use of the word  philosophize they begin to have a love  of wisdom a desire for wisdom they carry  out the activity  trying to attain wisdom it's not all  simply the experience of so oh my gosh  this is so amazing that sort of thing  it's also being as we could say  perplexed or finding ourselves unable to  provide an account unable to explain  something and so he also uses in the  verbal and also the noun form this term  upper area and this is a word that the  ancient Greeks use it literally means  being hemmed in you you go into  something and there's no way out there's  no path to get out of there you could go  backwards but then you're not achieving  anything so you're kind of stuck and we  have this experience many times when we  are wandering when we are marveling we  say how does that happen  what what's going on there and we're not  able to provide ourselves with an answer  that satisfies so that motivates us this  healing this effective experience which  is also cognitive because we feel  ourselves as he says to be ignorant  literally the verb to not know to not  have knowledge of something and that  leads us then to desire and to pursue  knowledge to try to get ourselves out of  the state of ignorance and into a state  of knowing something to be the case of  understanding it and this is something  that we not only think to ourselves we  desire it we pursue it this is how we  orient ourselves practically he gives us  some interesting examples about what  this might encompass before that though  he also tells us very importantly that  we wonder not only about these things  but we wonder or we're in awe or were  marveling at the fact that things are as  they are in the way that they are in  Greek who toasts okay that they they  hold themselves or are conditioned or  are structured or appear in a certain  manner to us and this is going beyond  just the sheer fact isset e of things  are the way they are we want to  understand how they are and we also want  to understand a much more important  question that's at the very core of  metaphysics why are things the way they  are what's going on there with each of  these examples now he gives us some  great examples one of which I'm going to  come back to a little bit later I'm  gonna skip over the first one for the  moment one of these is changes as he  says or motions of the Sun and the moon  and the stars the the things that we can  observe above and you know what would  this include you know the solstices he  has an example there probably you would  also say eclipses are things that we're  interested in the regular patterns that  we observe with heavenly bodies all of  those sorts of things are matters about  which we can wonder and he says that you  know mythmakers are actually in a  certain way beginning to do philosophy  because they introduce all sorts of  wonders for us and think about what some  of the myths in nearly every culture  have to do with the astral or heavenly  bodies we want to know what's going on  with these why are they behaving the way  that they do what is the Sun is it a  ball of fire is a kind of rock what  what's going on with that that's the  sort of thing that gets us to start  doing philosophy he also talks about a  mathematical example the  incommensurability  of the diagonal of the square now what  does that mean well you don't think  about a square the diagonal is the line  running through it talking about the  incommensurability means that you  can't find a ratio between the sides  that actually translates into to that  particular number there it was it was a  problem that we don't really have in  modern modern mathematics or geometry  because we understand how squares work  and how to work with them but for the  ancients this was very perplexing this  was a problem set for them that they  wanted to make some sense of and he says  something really interesting about that  particular example he says that it seems  wonderful or amazing to everyone who has  not yet perceived the cause that a thing  should not be measurable by the smallest  unit that's what the incommensurability  means but he says for a geometry  actually does understand it it would it  would be incredible it would be  something to wonder at if the diagonal  were suddenly to become measurable they  would want to know how did this happen  right now another thing that he talks  about about which scholars don't always  agree on the exact translation or  reference is the automata and you'll see  this often translated in the metaphysics  as marionettes what a lot of scholars  think these are would be in effect toys  or models perhaps of heavenly phenomena  perhaps of you know animals or or other  things that moved on their own so think  not quite so advanced but think of like  windup toys think of a mouse for example  where you wind it up and because of the  the spring action it makes wheels roll  and a cat chases if the cat thinks that  it's a self moving thing a mouse they  weren't quite so advanced as that in  terms of you know that type of  technology but they certainly did have  things like that in Aristotle's time and  so that's probably what he's referring  to here we don't really know exactly  what this  hurm signifies but that's a good guess  and if you were an ordinary person  seeing for example some mechanical toy  or seeing some sort of model of the  universe with with spheres that rotate  you might say whoa what is this thing  what what's going on here and then you  would start to think about it you would  want to move from a state of wonder and  perplexity to a state of knowledge and  philosophy would do that for you the  last example I want to talk about is one  that's no longer just you could say in  the physical world but has incredible  metaphysical import Aristotle says that  one of the other things that we wonder  about that leads us into studying  philosophy is the origins of everything  and he he is using those those exact  terms there you know it's a the pond  tastes every single thing and here we go  kai pantry tastes hoop on toast Ganesh  AOS Canaan tales is the genitive of  Genesis the word we translate in English  as Genesis or origin so the beginnings  or the origins or the coming to be of  everything that is a philosophical  question as well that can provoke wonder  why is everything the way it is how did  all of this come to be  so this is where philosophy begins  according to Aristotle in metaphysics  book 1  you
 

